# tries to read these as regular yaml files and they are all some sort of template
.goreleaser.plugin.tpl.yaml
docs/investigation/configuration/assets/communicationsecret.yaml
# helm/**
megalinter-reports/**
examples/**
test/**

# KSV-0041: ClusterRole botkube-clusterrole requires access to secrets in order to watch/list
# Kubernetes resources for alerting. This is intentional and documented behavior.
KSV-0041

# KSV-0046: ClusterRole botkube-plugins-default requires broad resource access to support
# the configurable plugin executor permissions. Users configure this via rbac.groups in values.yaml.
KSV-0046

# KSV-0048/KSV-0049/KSV-0113: The botkube-system Role requires access to pods, deployments,
# configmaps, and secrets to manage plugin lifecycle, runtime configuration, and read credentials.
# This is required functionality.
KSV-0048
KSV-0049
KSV-0113

# KSV-0011/KSV-0015/KSV-0016/KSV-0018: Resource limits/requests are intentionally not set in
# chart defaults since they vary significantly by deployment environment and workload.
# Users should set appropriate values via the resources: section in values.yaml.
KSV-0011
KSV-0015
KSV-0016
KSV-0018

# KSV-0020/KSV-0021: runAsUser/runAsGroup is set to 101 (botkube user) in values.yaml.
# The > 10000 threshold is an arbitrary hardening guideline, not a requirement.
KSV-0020
KSV-0021

# KSV-0110: Deployment namespace is set at Helm install time via --namespace flag.
# The default namespace in values.yaml is "default" as a placeholder; actual deployments
# use a dedicated namespace.
KSV-0110

# KSV-0125: The botkube image uses the project's own registry (ghcr.io/kubeshop).
# This is not an "untrusted" registry; Trivy's registry trust list doesn't include it.
KSV-0125

# DS-0026: HEALTHCHECK is intentionally omitted from these Dockerfiles.
# build/Dockerfile runs a long-lived agent that is monitored via Kubernetes liveness probes.
# build/Dockerfile.config_exporter runs a short-lived batch job and HEALTHCHECK is not applicable.
DS-0026

# github.com/mattermost/mattermost/server/v8 is used as a client library, not as a server.
# These CVEs are server-side vulnerabilities that require running a Mattermost server and
# are not exploitable via client library usage. Additionally, the pseudo-version
# v8.0.0-20260224033600-4b8ff4e6a3fd is a 2026-02-24 snapshot that postdates these fixes;
# Trivy incorrectly flags it because pseudo-versions compare as v8.0.0.
